<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/08-&#10;可编程生命材料/full&#10;Topic: Research question: How can matter be programmed&#10;into living materials?&#10;Context: Synthetic biology is an interdisciplinary field&#10;at the nexus of biochemistry, materials science and&#10;engineering, biology, chemistry, and other disciplines.&#10;Synthetic biologists aim to redesign or synthesize cells,&#10;life science systems, and even whole organisms to address&#10;grand challenges across sectors such as medicine and&#10;manufacturing. To engineer a new biological component,&#10;researchers combine DNA synthesis and genomics to assemble&#10;novel genomes. Self-assembly of nonliving and living&#10;components is another route being investigated, through&#10;both bottom-up and top-down processes. Wil Srubar, a&#10;materials scientist and architectural engineer, has been&#10;researching ways to embed cells with new abilities,&#10;“demonstrating the power and potential of engineered&#10;living materials at many scales, including electrically&#10;conductive biofilms, single-cell living catalysts for&#10;polymerization reactions, and living photovoltaics,” he&#10;writes.&#10;Status: ok&#10;Failed step: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="492" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: How can matter be programmed&#10;into living materials?&#10;Context: Synthetic biology is an interdisciplinary field&#10;at the nexus of biochemistry, materials science and&#10;engineering, biology, chemistry, and other disciplines.&#10;Synthetic biologists aim to redesign or synthesize cells,&#10;life science systems, and even whole organisms to address&#10;grand challenges across sectors such as medicine and&#10;manufacturing. To engineer a new biological component,&#10;researchers combine DNA synthesis and genomics to assemble&#10;novel genomes. Self-assembly of nonliving and living&#10;components is another route being investigated, through&#10;both bottom-up and top-down processes. Wil Srubar, a&#10;materials scientist and architectural engineer, has been&#10;researching ways to embed cells with new abilities,&#10;“demonstrating the power and potential of engineered&#10;living materials at many scales, including electrically&#10;conductive biofilms, single-cell living catalysts for&#10;polymerization reactions, and living photovoltaics,” he&#10;writes.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1212" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: completed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Preparation of Hybrid Sol-Gel&#10;Materials Based on Living Cells of&#10;Microorganisms and Their&#10;Application in Nanotechnology&#10;year: 2022&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4220814513&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Photodegradable Hydrogel Matrices&#10;for Spatiotemporal Control of&#10;Bacteria Transport and Delivery&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4413929537&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Metabolite-responsive Control of&#10;Transcription by Phase Separation-&#10;based Synthetic Organelles&#10;year: 2024&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4402617058&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;PenTag, a Versatile Platform for&#10;Synthesizing Protein-Polymer&#10;Biohybrid Materials&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4377098401&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 6460&#10;trend_chars: 10568" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: How can matter be&#10;programmed into living materials? Context: Synthetic biology&#10;is an interdisciplinary field at the nexus of biochemistry,&#10;materials science and engineering, biology, chemistry, and&#10;other disciplines. Synthetic biologists aim to redesign or&#10;synthesize cells, life science systems, and even whole&#10;organisms to address grand challenges across sectors such as&#10;medicine and manufacturing. To engineer a new biological&#10;component, researchers combine DNA synthesis and genomics to&#10;assemble novel genomes. Self-assembly of nonliving and living&#10;components is another route being investigated, through both&#10;bottom-up and top-down processes. Wil Srubar, a materials&#10;scientist and architectural engineer, has been researching&#10;ways to embed cells with new abilities, “demonstrating the&#10;power and potential of engineered living materials at many&#10;scales, including electrically conductive biofilms, single-&#10;cell living catalysts for polymerization reactions, and living&#10;photovoltaics,” he writes. seed_paper: Preparation of Hybrid&#10;Sol-Gel Materials Based on Living Cells of Microorganisms and&#10;Their Application in Nanotechnology # CITATION SUBGRAPH (3&#10;refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1] Photodegradable&#10;Hydrogel Matrices for Spatiotemporal Control of Bacteria&#10;Transport and Delivery (2025, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE]&#10;layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 1&#10;section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown is_influential =&#10;False low_confidence = True cited_by_subgraph = 0&#10;[PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[] delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none&#10;[IDEA] Problem: Stimuli-responsive hydrogels that provide&#10;controlled degradation can be used as bacteria delivery&#10;systems for advanced therapeutic applications. Here, we report&#10;the first use of photodegradable hydrogels as materials that&#10;can direct bacterial movement, tune mean bacteria speed, and&#10;control bacteria Existing Methods: Unavailable from local&#10;metadata. Motivation: Stimuli-responsive hydrogels that&#10;provide controlled degradation can be used as bacteria&#10;delivery systems for advanced therapeutic applications. Here,&#10;we report the first use of photodegradable hydrogels as&#10;materials that can direct bacterial movement, tune mean&#10;bacteria speed, and control bacteria Proposed Method: Stimuli-&#10;responsive hydrogels that provide controlled degradation can&#10;be used as bacteria delivery systems for advanced therapeutic&#10;applications. Here, we report the first use of photodegradable&#10;hydrogels as materials that can direct bacterial movement,&#10;tune mean bacteria speed, and control bacteria Experiment&#10;Plan: Unavailable from local metadata. [ABSTRACT] Stimuli-&#10;responsive hydrogels that provide controlled degradation can&#10;be used as bacteria delivery systems for advanced therapeutic&#10;applications. Here, we report the first use of photodegradable&#10;hydrogels as materials that can direct bacterial movement,&#10;tune mean bacteria speed, and control bacteria delivery&#10;through ... ## [2] Metabolite-responsive Control of&#10;Transcription by Phase Separation-based Synthetic Organelles&#10;(2024, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1&#10;cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 0 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unk...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: I interpret the three papers in chronological&#10;scientific order — Paper 0 = PenTag, 2023; Paper 1 =&#10;metabolite-responsive phase-separation synthetic organelles,&#10;2024; Paper 2 = photodegradable hydrogel matrices, 2025 —&#10;because that ordering best reveals the conceptual evolution&#10;from molecular biohybrid construction to cellular regulation&#10;to spatiotemporal control of living cells in materials. Paper&#10;0 to Paper 1: PenTag represents an early-stage enabling&#10;technology for programming matter with biological function.&#10;Its central contribution is the site-specific, covalent&#10;attachment of proteins to solid supports and hydrogels,&#10;addressing a persistent problem in biohybrid materials: how to&#10;connect biological components to nonliving matter in a stable,&#10;specific, and useful way. This is essentially a molecular&#10;“wiring” problem. If matter is to become living or&#10;biologically programmable, researchers first need reliable&#10;methods to attach enzymes, receptors, structural proteins, or&#10;other functional biomolecules to synthetic matrices without&#10;nonspecific binding or unstable non-covalent interactions. The&#10;transition to the 2024 work on metabolite-responsive, phase-&#10;separation-based synthetic organelles marks a shift from&#10;static bioconjugation toward dynamic biological information&#10;processing. Here, the emphasis is no longer only on attaching&#10;a protein to a material, but on creating intracellular&#10;structures that can sense metabolic states and regulate&#10;transcription. This moves the field closer to “programming”&#10;living matter from within: the material is not merely&#10;functionalized with biology, but begins to exhibit life-like&#10;sensing and adaptation. Phase separation provides a route to&#10;organize biochemical reactions inside cells, while metabolite&#10;responsiveness links material behavior to cellular physiology.&#10;The advance matters because it transforms biohybrid materials&#10;from passive composites into systems capable of internal&#10;feedback and adaptive response. Paper 1 to Paper 2: The 2025&#10;photodegradable hydrogel work shifts the locus of control from&#10;intracellular regulation to extracellular, spatiotemporal&#10;programming of living cells. Rather than focusing primarily on&#10;how cells internally sense and respond, this work uses a&#10;light-responsive material matrix to direct bacterial movement,&#10;tune bacterial speed, and control delivery. This is a major&#10;conceptual step: the nonli..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2040" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3108" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Engineered living materials and synthetic&#10;biology for programming nonliving matter with living cellular&#10;functions&#10;&#10;core_gap: Current methods separately achieve protein-material&#10;conjugation, intracellular metabolite-responsive regulation,&#10;and light-controlled cell transport, but they do not provide a&#10;unified, predictive, closed-loop programming framework that&#10;coordinates molecular interfacing, cellular sensing, genetic&#10;regulation, and extracellular matrix remodeling into a single&#10;living material system while preserving viability,&#10;orthogonality, and spatiotemporal control.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- living cells&#10;- microorganisms&#10;- bacteria&#10;- proteins&#10;- solid supports&#10;- hydrogels&#10;- sol-gel materials&#10;- polymer matrices&#10;- biohybrid materials&#10;- synthetic organelles&#10;- phase-separated compartments&#10;- metabolites&#10;- transcriptional regulation&#10;- photodegradable matrices&#10;- light stimuli&#10;- spatiotemporal control fields&#10;- cell motility&#10;- cell delivery&#10;- genetic circuits&#10;- material degradation&#10;- material remodeling&#10;- bottom-up synthetic biology&#10;- top-down material fabrication&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- proteins are site-specifically and covalently conjugated to&#10;solid supports or hydrogels to form biohybrid materials&#10;- synthetic organelles use phase separation to organize&#10;intracellular biochemical reactions&#10;- metabolite-responsive synthetic organelles translate&#10;metabolic cues into transcriptional control&#10;- photodegradable hydrogel matrices translate light input into&#10;controlled material degradation&#10;- controlled material degradation directs bacterial movement,&#10;tunes bacterial speed, and controls delivery&#10;- nonliving material matrices act as instruction media for&#10;living cells&#10;- bottom-up synthetic biology reconstructs life-like sensing&#10;and adaptation in materials&#10;- top-down material processing imposes spatial architecture&#10;and external control on living materials&#10;- molecular bioconjugation connects biological function to&#10;nonliving matter&#10;- intracellular sensing links cellular physiology to material&#10;behavior&#10;- extracellular stimuli link external user control to cellular&#10;behavior&#10;- local biological events are intended to scale into&#10;macroscopic material function&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- nonspecific binding and unstable non-covalent conjugation&#10;between biological and nonbiological components&#10;- loss of biological activity, specificity, or viability&#10;during material processing and actuation&#10;- lack of robust design rules connecting molecular events to&#10;cell behavior and bulk material properties&#10;- limited stimulus modalities and control precision, including&#10;light penetration depth, resolution, phototoxicity, heating,&#10;and degradation byproducts&#10;- metabolite-based control may lack precise external&#10;addressability&#10;- separation between intracellular sensing and extracellular&#10;material-mediated control&#10;- absence of closed-loop integration among sensing,&#10;computation, and material remodeling&#10;- difficulty balancing stable cell containment with&#10;controllable release, movement, secretion, or degradation&#10;- difficulty combining bottom-up biological adaptation with&#10;top-down fabricated structure&#10;- long-term instability caused by mutation, drift, death, or&#10;phenotype change in living components&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- autonomous biological responsiveness versus externally&#10;programmable control&#10;- high-resolution spatiotemporal actuation versus biological&#10;benignness and cell viability&#10;- stable containment of living cells versus programmable&#10;release and function&#10;- bottom-up life-like adaptation versus top-down architectural&#10;precision&#10;- long-term material stability versus beneficial biological&#10;evolvability and self-repair&#10;- multi-input responsiveness versus orthogonal control without&#10;crosstalk&#10;- molecular-scale bioconjugation versus macroscopic material&#10;behavior&#10;- intracellular regulation versus extracellular transport and&#10;delivery control&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- programmable translation of functional goals into biological&#10;and material design choices&#10;- stable and specific interfacing between proteins, cells,&#10;genetic components, and synthetic matrices&#10;- preservation of cell viability and metabolic function during&#10;fabrication and operation&#10;- closed-loop coupling of sensing, regulation, and material&#10;remodeling&#10;- orthogonal response to multiple stimuli without crosstalk&#10;- spatiotemporal control in three-dimensional materials with&#10;high precision and low toxicity&#10;- predictive multiscale behavior linking molecular events,&#10;cellular responses, and bulk material properties&#10;- controllable containment, release, and localization of&#10;living components&#10;- compatibility with both bottom-up synthetic biology and top-&#10;down material fabrication&#10;- long-term functional stability with controlled adaptability&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- site-specific covalent bioconjugation mechanisms&#10;- stimulus-responsive degradation and remodeling mechanisms&#10;- phase-separated synthetic organelle mechanisms&#10;- metabolite-responsive transcriptional control mechanisms&#10;- light-gated or photochemical actuation mechanisms&#10;- closed-loop biological feedback control mechanisms&#10;- orthogonal multi-input signal transduction mechanisms&#10;- spatiotemporal cell-guidance and delivery mechanisms&#10;- compartmentalization mechanisms for isolating biochemical&#10;reactions&#10;- multiscale design-rule mechanisms linking molecular&#10;programming to macroscopic material behavior&#10;- containment-and-release mechanisms balancing localization&#10;and biological activity&#10;- hybrid bottom-up/top-down fabrication mechanisms&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- developmental biology mechanisms for morphogen-gradient-&#10;based spatiotemporal patterning&#10;- extracellular matrix remodeling mechanisms in tissues&#10;- biofilm formation, dispersal, and containment strategies&#10;- phase separation and biomolecular condensate organization in&#10;cells&#10;- synthetic gene circuit design for orthogonal sensing and&#10;feedback&#10;- control theory and cybernetic feedback systems for closed-&#10;loop regulation&#10;- photopharmacology and deep-tissue light delivery for benign&#10;optical control&#10;- soft matter physics for stimulus-responsive phase&#10;transitions&#10;- 3D bioprinting and microfabrication for top-down living-&#10;material architecture&#10;- microfluidic gradient generators for controlled cell&#10;transport and environmental cueing&#10;- protein engineering for specific covalent material anchoring&#10;- synthetic ecology for stable multi-cellular division of&#10;labor" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3108" width="560" height="3192" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6400" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: The problem requires same-field retrieval to ground&#10;solutions in engineered living materials, bioconjugation,&#10;synthetic biology, hydrogels, and photodegradable matrices.&#10;Cross-domain retrieval is also needed because key&#10;bottlenecks—closed-loop control, orthogonal multi-input&#10;regulation, spatiotemporal patterning, containment-release&#10;balance, and multiscale design rules—suggest transfer of&#10;mechanisms from developmental biology, control theory, soft&#10;matter, biofilm ecology, photopharmacology, or&#10;microfabrication." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6400" width="560" height="348" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6848" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Faculty Opinions recommendation of Spatiotemporal&#10;control of cell signalling using a light-switchable protein&#10;interaction.&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Genetically-encodable optical reporters, such as&#10;Green Fluorescent Protein, have revolutionized the observation&#10;and measurement of cellular states. However, the inverse&#10;challenge of using light to precisely control cellular&#10;behavior has only recently begun to be addressed; sem...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Editorial: Insights in nanobiotechnology 2024/2025:&#10;novel developments, current challenges, and future&#10;perspectives&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Nanobiotechnology is progressing rapidly, and the&#10;last few years have brought a noticeable shift in what the&#10;community expects as proof. Advances in nanomaterial design,&#10;characterization, and experimental models now make it possible&#10;to ask more demanding questions: not only wh...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Towards Big Biology:: high-performance verification of&#10;large concurrent systems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Formal Verification for Natural and Engineered&#10;Biological Systems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Computational modeling is now used effectively to&#10;complement experimental work in biology, allowing to identify&#10;gaps in our understanding of the biological systems studied,&#10;and to predict system behavior based on a mechanistic model.We&#10;provide an overview of several areas in b...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: control_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Systems and control theoretic approaches to engineer&#10;robust biological systems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: control_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: A computational framework for optimal and Model&#10;Predictive Control of stochastic gene regulatory networks&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Engineering biology requires precise control of&#10;biomolecular circuits, and Cybergenetics is the field&#10;dedicated to achieving this goal. A significant challenge in&#10;developing controllers for cellular functions is designing&#10;systems that can effectively manage molecular noise. To..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fde9d9;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6848" width="620" height="1302" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step6_candidates_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step6_candidates_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step7" value="Step 7 Analogy Extractio